WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.830
In the early 1920s, a businessman in Boston
had a realization: everybody wants to get

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.459
rich, without doing any work.

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:13.949
So this businessman came up with a brilliant
idea to make a LOT of money.

00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:18.680
And, like all good financiers, he promised
to make those around him really rich too.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.400
The businessman told his investors he was
buying discounted coupons in foreign countries

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.760
and making money reselling them in the US.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.550
It was a middleman opportunity that he claimed
was totally safe and guaranteed a return.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:37.090
The businessman promised his investors a 50
percent profit in 45 days, or a 100 percent

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.390
profit in 90 days.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.480
What this businessman was actually doing was
paying the people who got in early with the

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.700
money from the people who got in late.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.050
Those later investors, excited by hearing
about a guaranteed high-return investment,

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.040
were left holding the bag.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.730
That businessman was named Charles Ponzi.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.270
His name has become synonymous with these
sorts of swindles.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.640
His “Ponzi scheme” made millions of dollars
in just a few months.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.420
But the con could only go on for so long.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.950
Eventually, you run out of other people’s
money.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.830
And for Ponzi, when that happens, you go to
jail.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.420
A hundred years later, a new bunch of wealthy
investors say they too have a brilliant way

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.960
to make YOU a lot of money.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.650
They promise to make you really, really rich.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:22.020
The best part is, they say, the returns are
guaranteed!

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.360
Anyone can buy in with any amount of money.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.360
It’s called Bitcoin.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.360
Sounds fun, right?

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.869
Bitcoin is a digital token, with no physical
or government backing, that’s run by a global,

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:33.250
decentralized network of computers.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:37.640
These computers keep track of every Bitcoin
transaction, and can move Bitcoins easily

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.630
from one user to another.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.780
There are a limited number of Bitcoins in
the world – 21 million.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.520
19 million of these have already been “mined.”

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.810
These “mining rigs” are large systems
of computers which perform complex calculations,

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.079
expending huge amounts of energy, to create
new bitcoins.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.860
Massive Bitcoin mining “farms” are set
up in places like Siberia and Northeastern

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.860
China, where people take advantage of cheap
electricity in massive, wasteful facilities.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.869
But how Bitcoin works doesn’t really matter
that much.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:09.440
All of the stuff you hear about “peer-to-peer
technology” and “the blockchain” really

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.260
only explains how Bitcoin works as a CURRENCY.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.480
But despite the name, Bitcoin is not really
used much as a currency at all.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.200
When people buy Bitcoin, they rarely use it
to buy goods and services.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.590
They’re really buying access to a Ponzi
scheme.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.059
Only 1.3 percent of Bitcoin’s economic activity
is in merchant transactions.

00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:32.470
The rest is SPECULATION – people buying
it just because they think it will go up.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.090
And the more it’s used in speculation, the
less incentive people have to actually use

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.680
it as a currency.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.330
Why buy something with Bitcoin when you can
hold it and make some easy money?

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:47.329
That’s why the peak of Bitcoin being used
to buy things was actually back in 2017, before

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:48.719
the price really took off.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:53.699
And, a currency should be stable in value,
not bounce all over the place the way bitcoin

00:02:53.699 --> 00:02:54.699
does.

00:02:54.699 --> 00:02:57.019
Rises are nice, but Bitcoin also falls a lot.

00:02:57.019 --> 00:02:59.640
If you want to speculate, that’s your own
business.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.689
But you don’t want the value of your checking
account to gyrate wildly and unpredictably

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:06.129
just because Elon Musk tweets about it.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.629
If you have friends or relatives who are into
cryptocurrency, you know that the reason people

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.890
are into Bitcoin is because they think it
can make them a profit.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.250
Because Bitcoin is just a digital token without
any inherent value, they’re not really making

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.639
an “investment”: they’re engaged in
a financial betting game.

00:03:22.639 --> 00:03:26.900
Anything you “invest” in bitcoin (or ethereum,
or dogecoin) just goes into the pocket of

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.579
the previous holder—there’s no real income-generating
asset behind it.

00:03:30.579 --> 00:03:34.239
What they’re betting on is that other people
will bet on it, and the value will keep going

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:35.239
up.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:38.040
If more people are joining the market, you
CAN make money.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.400
You’re not a sucker if the person next in
line is a sucker too.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.480
And as long as you can make it out on time,
the next guy is left holding the bag.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.170
The good news for these gamblers is that there
are a lot of suckers in the world.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.980
The bad news is that eventually, you run out
of suckers.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.709
So to keep the scam running as long as they
can, the wealthy proponents of Bitcoin say

00:03:58.709 --> 00:03:59.999
that it’s “changing the world.”

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:04.459
They say that Bitcoin is going to replace
“fiat currencies,” like the dollar.

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:08.680
They say that it’s going to undermine cruel
dictatorships and greedy banks, and help usher

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.129
in a libertarian utopia of privacy and security.

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:16.419
And, most importantly, they say what every
scam artist has said since the beginning of

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:22.970
time: get in right now, and you’ll make
quick and easy money.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.360
And for the last few years, they’ve actually
been right.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.460
If you had spent that money on buying shares
of Apple, you’d have about five hundred

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.949
and forty bucks.

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:36.370
If you spent $100 on Bitcoin in January 2016,
you’d have made more than nine thousand

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:37.370
dollars.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.730
But that doesn’t mean that Bitcoin is working.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:43.800
It just means that a lot of people have fallen
for the scam.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.460
And the more people who buy in, the more the
price fluctuates, and the less usable it is

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.819
as a currency.

00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:53.560
The success of Bitcoin as a speculative commodity
has made it socially useless.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:54.560
Anything can fill the role of that scam currency.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.909
You’d actually have made just as much money
buying a parody cryptocurrency called Dogecoin

00:04:58.909 --> 00:05:03.900
that makes none of the grandiose claims that
Bitcoin does.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.069
Meanwhile, as more and more people speculate
on Bitcoin, it consumes more and more electricity,

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:11.680
and emits more and more carbon.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.969
Because of how complex Bitcoin transactions
are, they consume an incredible amount of

00:05:15.969 --> 00:05:17.180
power.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.349
One Bitcoin transaction consumes more electricity
than the average American household does in

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:23.349
a month.

00:05:23.349 --> 00:05:28.360
Bitcoin as a whole consumes more electricity
than Venezuela, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Kazakhstan,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.990
and the Philippines.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:34.939
If Bitcoin ever WERE to be widely adopted,
it would consume many times more energy than

00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:36.900
is produced by the entire world right now.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.430
Bitcoin isn’t useful at all, except maybe
as a symbol of how capitalism has changed:

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.840
from something that could once build factories
and develop the country, to a system of pure

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.889
speculation totally divorced from the real
world.

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:56.129
Look at this system: millions of computers
emitting huge amounts of carbon, all to gain

00:05:56.129 --> 00:06:01.069
access to a digital token with no actual use,
all while infrastructure crumbles and people

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:02.069
go hungry.

00:06:02.069 --> 00:06:04.460
This is a terrible way to organize a society.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.530
But just because Bitcoin is a Ponzi scheme
without any benefit to society does not mean

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:10.530
it’s going away.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:15.300
Bitcoin has become a CULTURE, an IDENTITY:
amid the vast meaninglessness of a society

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.390
in decline, a lot of people find value in
it.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:22.770
In a world where traditional forms of identity
are in decline – family, unions, religion

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:26.500
– new ones, like cryptocurrencies, spring
up to take their place.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.749
The fact that so many lonely, atomized people
find community and meaning in Bitcoin means

00:06:30.749 --> 00:06:34.909
that for the people at the very top – people
with millions or even billions of dollars

00:06:34.909 --> 00:06:38.430
– there’s a ready and willing community
of suckers to exploit.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.710
You can still make money off cryptocurrencies.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:46.599
In an age of low wages and mass underemployment,
people will ALWAYS be lured by the promise

00:06:46.599 --> 00:06:48.419
of getting rich quick.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:51.630
But there will always be somebody left holding
the bag.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:52.919
And it could very well be you.

00:06:52.919 --> 00:06:55.849
In essence, Bitcoin is a gamble.

00:06:55.849 --> 00:06:58.279
Gambling can be fun at low stakes with friends.

00:06:58.279 --> 00:07:01.190
But the people trying to sell you Bitcoin
aren’t your friends.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:06.120
No matter what you tell yourself, you’re
just gambling – not that an asset has value,

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.400
but that the person next in line is more gullible
than you.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.310
This is Doug Henwood, author of Wall Street,
for the Gravel Institute.

